  "content": {
    "id": "p57-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Write an equation of a line.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p57-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "perpendicular to $y = 0.75x + 5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y = -\\frac{4}{3}x + c$, where $c$ can be any number.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{\\mathrm{orig}} &= 0.75 = \\frac{3}{4}\\\\m_{\\mathrm{perp}} &= -\\frac{4}{3}\\\\y &= -\\frac{4}{3}x + c\\end{align*}$$\nThe perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal. No point is specified, so $c$ can be any number.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "perpendicular to $y = 0.75x + 5$ that passes through $(0,18)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y = -\\frac{4}{3}x + 18$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}m_{\\mathrm{orig}} &= 0.75 = \\frac{3}{4}\\\\m_{\\mathrm{perp}} &= -\\frac{4}{3}\\\\c_{\\mathrm{perp}} &= 18\\\\y &= -\\frac{4}{3}x + 18\\end{align*}$$\nThe perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal, and $(0,18)$ gives the $y$-intercept.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Explain why there are infinitely many answers to part **a**, but only one answer to part **b**.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Part a has no specific point, so any $y$-intercept $c$ gives a perpendicular line. Part b requires passing through a specific point, which determines $c$ uniquely.",
          "worked": "Both parts require gradient $-\\frac{4}{3}$. In part a, the $y$-intercept can be any number, giving infinitely many lines. In part b, passing through $(0,18)$ fixes the $y$-intercept at $18$, giving exactly one line.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 21
      }
    ]
  },
